From udadcb4fd6a01f8d1 at groups.google.com  Wed Aug 26 15:58:35 2020
From: udadcb4fd6a01f8d1 at groups.google.com (Chris Watson)
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2020 15:58:35 +0000
Subject: Re: [brainGraph-help] Best way of computing graph attributes by lobe
Message-ID: <h87Cy3tbqyY.OQ8vYh0BCwAJ@braingraph-help.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <h87Cy3tbqyY.oSw1xXvcCgAJ@braingraph-help.groups.google.com>
References: <h87Cy3tbqyY.oSw1xXvcCgAJ@braingraph-help.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/braingraph-help/c/h87Cy3tbqyY/m/OQ8vYh0BCwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It depends on what you want to compute, exactly.
* If you are interested in a subnetwork consisting only of regions for a given lobe, then yes your proposed approach would be fine.
* If you want to be sure that inter-lobe connections are not ignored, then it would be better to construct the entire graphs and then create a data.table w/ vertex-level metrics, and calculate by lobe using data.table syntax. e.g., something like
DT <- vertex_attr_dt(g)
DT[, mean(E.nodal.wt), by=lobe]

Chris

